  "worked_solution": "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability mass function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $0\\le f(x)\\le 1$ for all $x$ in $S$\n\n- ${\\sum }_{x\\in S}f(x)=1$\n\nLet's check the above conditions for each of the given functions.\n\n- The function $f$ is *not* a valid probability mass function because it does not satisfy the second condition. The values of $f$ do not sum to $1:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{2}{3}=\\frac{7}{6}\\ne 1\n$$\n\n- The function $g$ is a valid probability mass function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n0\\le \\frac{1}{3},\\frac{2}{3}\\le 1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}=1.\n$$\n\n- The function $h$ is *not* a valid probability distribution because the value $h(3)=\\frac{3}{2}$ does not satisfy the first condition (it is not between $0$ and $1$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$g$ only.\"",
